# G040 — Packet Sniffer

*v2. See `packet-sniffer.md` for prose: passive observation as
distinct mode from active probing, `@breakdown` generalised to
network traffic, bidirectional connection keys, anomaly detection
as statistical `where`.*

---

## Choreographic Case: Network Health Observer

[network-observer @threshold-bytes-per-sec
    IN ORDER TO
        capture TCP traffic passively while the rest of the system runs
        summarise every minute, and escalate only on statistical signatures
        so the observer never participates, only measures

    @capture <- [sniffer[start {filter: {protocol: "tcp"}}]]

    every 60 seconds
        @stats <- [sniffer[summary {capture: @capture}]]
        @syn-suspects  <- [sniffer[detect-syn-flood
                                     {capture: @capture, threshold: 100}]]
        @scan-suspects <- [sniffer[detect-port-scan
                                     {capture: @capture, threshold: 20}]]

        @syn-suspects
            where count is greater than zero
                -> [alert[dispatch
                           {kind: "syn-flood",
                            sources: @syn-suspects}]]
        @scan-suspects
            where count is greater than zero
                -> [alert[dispatch
                           {kind: "port-scan",
                            sources: @scan-suspects}]]
        @stats
            where bytes-per-second is greater than @threshold-bytes-per-sec
                -> [alert[dispatch
                           {kind: "bandwidth",
                            rate: @stats:bytes-per-second}]]
]

---

## Verb Surface

[sniffer
    start               {filter}
    summary             {capture}
    breakdown           {capture, by}            ;; "protocol"|"src-ip"|"connection"
    detect-syn-flood    {capture, threshold}
    detect-port-scan    {capture, threshold}
    connections         {capture}
]

---

## Distinctive Innate idiom: bidirectional identity `where` endpoints sort

[sniffer[connection-key {src-ip: @a, src-port: @ap,
                          dst-ip: @b, dst-port: @bp}]
    IN ORDER TO
        treat A→B and B→A as one conversation
        so request and response coalesce under a single key

    sort [(@a, @ap), (@b, @bp)] by (ip, port)
    return the pair as a canonical ConnectionKey
]

Agents' request-response flows are inherently bidirectional; the
connection tracker normalises direction before counting. Same
discipline InnateScript's agent correspondence uses — a commission
and its return converge on one handle.

## Distinctive Innate idiom: anomaly as `where` over aggregate shape

[sniffer[detect-syn-flood {capture: @c, threshold: @t}]
    IN ORDER TO
        signal hostile behaviour from statistics, not packet content
        so cheap aggregate analysis gates expensive response logic

    group @c:packets by src-ip
        where syn-count is greater than @t
        and matching ack-count much less than syn-count
        -> append src-ip to suspects

    return suspects
]

The `where` evaluates the *aggregate* shape, not individual packets.
An agent that sends too many requests without processing responses
is the noosphere equivalent of a SYN flood.

---

## Demo

[demo
    @cap <- [sniffer[start {filter: {protocol: "tcp"}}]]
    ;; ... traffic flows ...

    -> [sniffer[breakdown {capture: @cap, by: "protocol"}]]
    ;; -> {TCP: 1523, UDP: 482, ICMP: 14}

    -> [sniffer[breakdown {capture: @cap, by: "connection"}]]
    ;; -> {((A:1234, B:80)): 47 packets, ...}

    -> [sniffer[detect-port-scan
                  {capture: @cap, threshold: 20}]]
    ;; -> ["10.0.0.5"]  — one source probed > 20 distinct dst-ports
]
